00:10:08,240 --> 00:10:09,740
It looks easy, but
actually they are prone

262
00:10:10,240 --> 00:10:11,740
to catching edges, which
would cause a spill.

263
00:10:11,740 --> 00:10:14,250
Greater speed is needed
for this stunt.

264
00:10:14,240 --> 00:10:16,240
Man, imagine what he could
do with a full set,

265
00:10:16,240 --> 00:10:17,740
a full size, huh?

266
00:10:18,750 --> 00:10:20,750
Here's Alfredo Mendoza,

267
00:10:20,750 --> 00:10:22,250
one of the greats in
water skiing.

268
00:10:22,250 --> 00:10:23,250
I know his skis.

269
00:10:23,250 --> 00:10:25,260
I'm gonna sniff myself!

270
00:10:25,250 --> 00:10:26,750
He doesn't need skis.

271
00:10:26,760 --> 00:10:28,760
His bare feet will do nicely.

272
00:10:28,760 --> 00:10:30,760
Oh, he's got webbed toes.

273
00:10:30,760 --> 00:10:31,760
He says it's easy

274
00:10:31,760 --> 00:10:32,260
if you don't wiggle your toes.

275
00:10:32,260 --> 00:10:33,260
Am I a clown?

276
00:10:33,760 --> 00:10:35,270
Do you find me amusing?

277
00:10:36,260 --> 00:10:38,760
There's only one way to stop:
just let go.

278
00:10:38,770 --> 00:10:40,270
Of your sensibility and
dress like a girl!

279
00:10:40,270 --> 00:10:41,770
You know you want to.

280
00:10:41,770 --> 00:10:43,270
This young lady is
showing us the type

281
00:10:43,270 --> 00:10:45,280
of ski used for the
single ski technique.

282
00:10:45,770 --> 00:10:47,270
That's Alfredo, campin' it up!

283
00:10:47,280 --> 00:10:49,780
Next is the regulation
or two ski type.

284
00:10:49,780 --> 00:10:52,280
It has less depth to the
fin on the bottom.

285
00:10:52,280 --> 00:10:54,290
And she has less depth as well.

286
00:10:54,280 --> 00:10:55,280
Hey, hey.

287
00:10:55,280 --> 00:10:56,780
Oops.

288
00:10:56,790 --> 00:10:57,790
And the third is
wider with no fin.

289
00:10:57,790 --> 00:10:59,290
She looks fine to me.

290
00:10:59,290 --> 00:11:00,290
It is used for
turning and jumping.

291
00:11:00,290 --> 00:11:02,290
Hey fellas, are you listening?

292
00:11:02,290 --> 00:11:05,300
It's the way she touches
that board, it's...

293
00:11:05,290 --> 00:11:07,790
Single ski technique,
while different,

294
00:11:07,800 --> 00:11:09,300
is just as easy to perform.

295
00:11:09,300 --> 00:11:11,300
It has more grace and style.

296
00:11:11,300 --> 00:11:12,300
I can see that.

297
00:11:12,800 --> 00:11:14,310
Allows the skier to operate
at a wider range.

298
00:11:14,800 --> 00:11:15,800
It's just a whole lot better,
okay?

299
00:11:15,800 --> 00:11:16,300
Trust me!

300
00:11:16,810 --> 00:11:17,810
We're sold.

301
00:11:19,310 --> 00:11:20,810
Let's watch Willa McGuire.

302
00:11:20,810 --> 00:11:21,810
Let's watch Willa Cather

303
00:11:21,810 --> 00:11:23,810
doing her baby
schnooks impression.

304
00:11:23,810 --> 00:11:26,820
Her artistry and
skill has no equal

305
00:11:26,820 --> 00:11:27,820
on the aquatic hickory.

306
00:11:27,820 --> 00:11:28,820
Hickory?

307
00:11:28,820 --> 00:11:30,320
Oh, it's Leona Helmsley!

308
00:11:30,320 --> 00:11:32,320
Taxes are for the little people.

309
00:11:32,320 --> 00:11:34,830
Harry, Harry, come get me up.

310
00:11:41,830 --> 00:11:43,330
I'm building the master race.

311
00:11:43,330 --> 00:11:44,340
Willa tries the impossible.

312
00:11:44,330 --> 00:11:47,330
A ski ballet with no
hand or foot hold.

313
00:11:51,840 --> 00:11:54,350
She should be home caring
for her family,

314
00:11:54,340 --> 00:11:55,840
but no, I'll do everything.

315
00:11:55,840 --> 00:11:57,840
You just keep up your
little ski ballet

316
00:11:57,850 --> 00:11:59,350
or whatever the heck
it is you do.

317
00:12:00,350 --> 00:12:01,850
Nice.

318
00:12:01,850 --> 00:12:03,350
A popular form of
modern water skiing

319
00:12:03,350 --> 00:12:05,360
is sometimes called the
romantic double.

320
00:12:05,350 --> 00:12:07,350
Hey, you come here often?

321
00:12:07,360 --> 00:12:09,860
Hey, kick those skis off,
gimme your foot.

322
00:12:09,860 --> 00:12:10,860
There you go.

323
00:12:10,860 --> 00:12:12,860
Plenty of room on top,
here you go.

324
00:12:12,860 --> 00:12:15,870
You are friendly, aren't you.
(chuckles)

325
00:12:15,860 --> 00:12:17,360
Well, just throw that
stuff in the back,

326
00:12:17,870 --> 00:12:19,870
I kind of live off my shoulders.

327
00:12:20,370 --> 00:12:21,370
Wee!

328
00:12:22,870 --> 00:12:24,380
Hey hey hey, get a boat.

329
00:12:24,370 --> 00:12:25,870
I mean, get a room.

330
00:12:25,870 --> 00:12:29,370
Now the two young lovers bask
in the afterglow.

331
00:12:29,380 --> 00:12:30,880
How about a kiss?

332
00:12:32,880 --> 00:12:33,380
No?

333
00:12:33,380 --> 00:12:33,880
No?

334
00:12:34,380 --> 00:12:35,380
Goodbye.

335
00:12:35,380 --> 00:12:36,880
Hah, you're shark food now,
baby!

336
00:12:36,890 --> 00:12:37,890
Oh that's nice.

337
00:12:37,890 --> 00:12:39,390
Here's hoping this romantic pair

338
00:12:39,390 --> 00:12:40,390
have a happier ending.

339
00:12:40,390 --> 00:12:41,890
See, she's doing it!

340
00:12:41,890 --> 00:12:42,890
Yeah!

341
00:12:45,390 --> 00:12:48,390
They gotta watch out, this
could lead to dancing.

342
00:12:48,400 --> 00:12:50,400
Bob and Connie
really enjoy life.

343
00:12:52,400 --> 00:12:54,410
Hey, victory at sea, Bob.

344
00:12:54,400 --> 00:12:55,400
Woo woo!

345
00:12:55,400 --> 00:12:56,900
Alfredo is warming up again.

346
00:12:56,910 --> 00:12:58,410
This time, it's for the jump.

347
00:12:58,410 --> 00:13:00,910
He holds the world's record of
well over a hundred feet

348
00:13:00,910 --> 00:13:02,410
in board jumping.

349
00:13:03,910 --> 00:13:05,420
I'm bored jumping.

350
00:13:05,410 --> 00:13:07,410
Let's watch him again
in slow motion.

351
00:13:07,420 --> 00:13:08,420
Notice the arms do not rotate

352
00:13:08,420 --> 00:13:10,420
like on snow ski jumping.

353
00:13:10,420 --> 00:13:12,920
They just snap clean away!

354
00:13:13,920 --> 00:13:17,430
(classical music)

355
00:13:17,430 --> 00:13:20,930
Oh, all of a sudden it's Sergia
Prokofiev playing.

356
00:13:20,930 --> 00:13:23,930
Thought it was Handel's Water
Music. (chuckles)

357
00:13:23,930 --> 00:13:25,940
Alfredo's next is called the
helicopter spin.

358
00:13:25,930 --> 00:13:28,430
He'll try to do a double spin.

359
00:13:28,440 --> 00:13:29,940
It's never been done before

360
00:13:29,940 --> 00:13:31,940
and it takes nerve to try.

361
00:13:31,940 --> 00:13:33,440
Oh, I can do that.

362
00:13:37,450 --> 00:13:37,950
Whoa.

363
00:13:38,950 --> 00:13:40,450
Almost, but not quite.

364
00:13:40,450 --> 00:13:42,950
Remember that agony of
defeat guy on ABC?

365
00:13:42,950 --> 00:13:44,460
That was him in the
summer months.

366
00:13:44,450 --> 00:13:45,450
Oh, he's summering.

367
00:13:45,450 --> 00:13:46,950
This time the Mexican
jumping bean

368
00:13:46,960 --> 00:13:48,460
will do a one and a half
spin on the ramp,

369
00:13:48,460 --> 00:13:51,460
landing backwards and completing
the other half on water.

370
00:13:51,460 --> 00:13:53,460
Now this time, the white
fascist narrator

371
00:13:53,460 --> 00:13:54,970
will make a racial slur!

372
00:13:56,460 --> 00:13:57,960
Oh, but what do I know?

373
00:13:57,970 --> 00:13:59,470
I'm only a fat pink announcer

374
00:13:59,470 --> 00:14:00,970
mowing down pretzels and
pinwheel cookies

375
00:14:00,970 --> 00:14:02,970
and trying to come to grips
with the tattered ends

376
00:14:02,970 --> 00:14:05,480
of a once promising life
gone horribly wrong.

377
00:14:05,470 --> 00:14:07,970
God, god, why? (sobs)

378
00:14:07,980 --> 00:14:09,480
It's just a movie.

379
00:14:13,480 --> 00:14:15,490
Alfredo doesn't like to
get his feet wet

380
00:14:15,480 --> 00:14:16,480
coming ashore.

381
00:14:16,480 --> 00:14:19,480
(plane noises)

382
00:14:20,490 --> 00:14:23,990
The triple straight jump
has crowd appeal.

383
00:14:25,490 --> 00:14:27,490
Once more for the cameraman.

384
00:14:27,500 --> 00:14:30,000
One for my baby and one more,
for the cameraman.

385
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:31,000
This is the shot he was after.

386
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:32,500
Where's the third fellow?

387
00:14:32,500 --> 00:14:35,010
He's chicken, never
jumped at all.

388
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:36,000
What's this?

389
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:37,500
He's forced his way into the
announcer's booth!

390
00:14:37,510 --> 00:14:39,010
What's that in his hand?

391
00:14:39,010 --> 00:14:40,010
Oh no, ah!

392
00:14:40,010 --> 00:14:41,510
(gun noises)

393
00:14:41,510 --> 00:14:44,010
Believe it or not, even
water skiers get down

394
00:14:44,010 --> 00:14:46,020
with that old bugaboo monotony.

395
00:14:46,010 --> 00:14:48,510
So they choke each other
with their tow ropes.

396
00:14:48,520 --> 00:14:50,020
(choking noises)

397
00:14:50,020 --> 00:14:51,020
When that happens,

398
00:14:51,020 --> 00:14:52,520
the boys play a little game.

399
00:14:52,520 --> 00:14:55,030
They call it the
Cypress Garden weave.

400
00:14:55,020 --> 00:14:57,520
(chuckles) They call it
the sure death.

401
00:14:57,530 --> 00:14:58,530
Well I hope they're
all pepped up,

402
00:14:58,530 --> 00:15:00,030
because I'm exhausted.

403
00:15:00,030 --> 00:15:02,530
Yes, waterskiing today
is a big sport,

404
00:15:02,530 --> 00:15:05,040
and here is the big sendoff
from the whole gang.

405
00:15:05,030 --> 00:15:06,530
V is for Victory!

406
00:15:06,530 --> 00:15:09,030
Buy war bonds where you
work or bank!

407
00:15:09,040 --> 00:15:10,040
Aw.

408
00:15:10,540 --> 00:15:14,040
(fun futuristic music)

409
00:15:16,710 --> 00:15:17,720
(old-timey music)

410
00:15:17,710 --> 00:15:18,710
♪ Come along with me

411
00:15:18,710 --> 00:15:20,210
♪ Maybe then you'll see

412
00:15:20,220 --> 00:15:21,720
♪ My harnesses and my nets

413
00:15:21,720 --> 00:15:23,720
♪ Hey, catching trouble,
you and me

414
00:15:23,720 --> 00:15:25,720
♪ Catching trouble,
baby you can't see

415
00:15:25,720 --> 00:15:27,730
♪ Catching trouble and
maybe a kiss or three

416
00:15:28,220 --> 00:15:29,720
(whistles)

417
00:15:30,230 --> 00:15:31,730
Catching Trouble?

418
00:15:31,730 --> 00:15:32,730
Hello everybody, this
is Ted Husing,

419
00:15:33,230 --> 00:15:35,230
and I'm very happy to again
present Sport Light's

420
00:15:35,230 --> 00:15:36,730
good friend Ross Allen

421
00:15:36,730 --> 00:15:37,740
Hi, Ross!

422
00:15:37,730 --> 00:15:39,230
A modern Tarzan.

423
00:15:39,230 --> 00:15:40,230
Oh, he's going golfing.

424
00:15:40,240 --> 00:15:41,740
Leaving his headquarters,

425
00:15:41,740 --> 00:15:43,240
headed for the mysterious and
fascinating Everglades,

426
00:15:43,240 --> 00:15:45,740
where he will pick up his
faithful Seminole Indian guide

427
00:15:45,740 --> 00:15:48,750
to aid him in catching a
bag of trouble.

428
00:15:48,740 --> 00:15:51,740
Ross also plays the part of Lil'
Abner in summer theatre.

429
00:15:51,750 --> 00:15:54,250
Well he's a foley artist in
the off season.

430
00:15:56,750 --> 00:15:58,260
In a log dugout canoe,

431
00:15:58,250 --> 00:15:59,750
they head into the
swamp country.

432
00:16:00,260 --> 00:16:01,760
Do not bring your evil here.

433
00:16:01,760 --> 00:16:03,260
His daily job is
catching live animals

434
00:16:03,260 --> 00:16:04,760
and reptiles for the zoos.

435
00:16:04,760 --> 00:16:05,760
But at night he does Streisand

436
00:16:05,760 --> 00:16:08,270
at the Bubble Room on Captiva.

437
00:16:08,260 --> 00:16:10,260
Well, this is a
different assignment.

438
00:16:10,270 --> 00:16:12,770
And a true depiction of
actually filling an order

439
00:16:12,770 --> 00:16:13,770
he recently received.

440
00:16:13,770 --> 00:16:15,270
- It said... - Kill
Colonel Kurtz.

441
00:16:15,270 --> 00:16:16,770
Catch immediately one
live bobcat,

442
00:16:16,770 --> 00:16:18,780
two cub black bears,

443
00:16:18,770 --> 00:16:20,770
and three six-foot
diamondback rattlers.

444
00:16:20,780 --> 00:16:22,280
And two hard-boiled eggs.

445
00:16:22,280 --> 00:16:23,280
(squawks)

446
00:16:23,280 --> 00:16:24,280
Make that three
hard-boiled eggs.

447
00:16:24,280 --> 00:16:25,280
They leave the dugout

448
00:16:25,280 --> 00:16:26,280
and go into the Glades afoot.

449
00:16:26,280 --> 00:16:28,290
With Emo Phillips in a dress!

450
00:16:28,280 --> 00:16:31,280
I was wondering if maybe I could
follow you into the woods

451
00:16:31,290 --> 00:16:35,290
and maybe we could see
some animals.

452
00:16:37,790 --> 00:16:39,290
Now folks, this has
all been leveled

453
00:16:39,290 --> 00:16:41,290
and turned into a golf course.

454
00:16:42,300 --> 00:16:44,300
Wild cat, let's go that way!

455
00:16:44,300 --> 00:16:45,800
Wild cat, I think I love you,

456
00:16:45,800 --> 00:16:47,810
but I wanna know for sure.

457
00:16:49,800 --> 00:16:51,300
While the cat's away...

458
00:16:51,310 --> 00:16:52,810
(chuckles)

459
00:16:54,310 --> 00:16:55,310
Whoa.

460
00:16:55,810 --> 00:16:57,820
Lens problem there.

461
00:16:58,810 --> 00:17:01,810
High and dry but not as safe
as an alley cat.

462
00:17:01,820 --> 00:17:03,820
(chuckles) That's a little joke
from me, Ted Husing,

463
00:17:03,820 --> 00:17:04,820
in the booth.

464
00:17:05,320 --> 00:17:06,320
Stay there, wild cat!

465
00:17:06,320 --> 00:17:07,830
You'll soon be coming down.

466
00:17:07,820 --> 00:17:09,820
Ross is no actor, as
you can see.

467
00:17:10,830 --> 00:17:12,830
Talk him down, Emo.

468
00:17:14,830 --> 00:17:15,830
Oops.

469
00:17:15,830 --> 00:17:17,330
That's one tree he
won't climb again.

470
00:17:17,330 --> 00:17:19,340
He lands on all four feet
and goes to town,

471
00:17:19,330 --> 00:17:20,830
but Ross keeps his eye on him

472
00:17:20,840 --> 00:17:22,340
and he's right on his heels.

473
00:17:22,340 --> 00:17:27,340
You know Ross is no stranger
to raping the land.

474
00:17:27,840 --> 00:17:29,340
Hey Mr. Cat, you can't do that.

475
00:17:29,340 --> 00:17:31,340
Don't you know you're
wanted in Chicago?

476
00:17:31,350 --> 00:17:33,850
For voting twice? (chuckles)

477
00:17:33,850 --> 00:17:35,850
Up he goes, and so goes Ross.

478
00:17:37,350 --> 00:17:39,860
That's a bad limb, hop to
the next one, pussy.

479
00:17:39,850 --> 00:17:40,850
Hey!

480
00:17:40,860 --> 00:17:41,860
(growling)

481
00:17:41,860 --> 00:17:42,860
He's talking about the cat.

482
00:17:42,860 --> 00:17:44,360
This is where my boyfriend
knows his stuff.

483
00:17:44,360 --> 00:17:46,860
Hey Ted, you don't have to say
anything more, Ted.

484
00:17:47,360 --> 00:17:48,370
If you think trying to smuggle

485
00:17:48,360 --> 00:17:49,860
a fighting 35-pound wild cat
into a flour bag

486
00:17:49,860 --> 00:17:51,860
as you balance yourself on a
limb is child's play,

487
00:17:51,870 --> 00:17:54,370
oh ho, you've got
another guess coming.

488
00:17:54,370 --> 00:17:58,380
Oh boy, yeah yowza, yes sir,
whatamatoo.

489
00:17:58,370 --> 00:17:59,870
Ho ho, 23 skidoo.

490
00:18:03,380 --> 00:18:03,880
Oh.

491
00:18:04,880 --> 00:18:06,380
Well, he's in the bag!

492
00:18:06,380 --> 00:18:08,890
And so am I, Ted Husing.

493
00:18:08,880 --> 00:18:09,880
Yes, go for it!

494
00:18:09,880 --> 00:18:11,880
Yay, the cat got away!

495
00:18:11,890 --> 00:18:13,890
The cat escapes north through
the Underground Railroad!

496
00:18:14,390 --> 00:18:18,400
And he just ripped a hunk of
skin off his inner thigh.

497
00:18:18,390 --> 00:18:20,390
Say, you made a mistake
picking that tree.

498
00:18:20,400 --> 00:18:22,400
I'm afraid you're out of
luck this time.

499
00:18:22,400 --> 00:18:24,900
Bite me, I will prevail!

500
00:18:24,900 --> 00:18:26,400
Mine is a noble race!

501
00:18:26,400 --> 00:18:28,410
(growling)

502
00:18:30,410 --> 00:18:32,410
Put some salt on his tail Mr.
Allen,

503
00:18:32,410 --> 00:18:34,410
and don't take any more
feline back talk!

504
00:18:34,410 --> 00:18:35,910
Well that's nice.

505
00:18:38,910 --> 00:18:39,910
Gee.

506
00:18:39,910 --> 00:18:41,910
Well it's in the bag,
and so Ross Allen

507
00:18:41,920 --> 00:18:43,920
fills one third of his
day's orders.

508
00:18:43,920 --> 00:18:44,920
What's next?

509
00:18:44,920 --> 00:18:46,420
Hurting the people you
know and love?

510
00:18:46,420 --> 00:18:48,930
Chasing rabbits on a minibike
until their hearts explode?

511
00:18:50,930 --> 00:18:52,930
Order number two was
soon at hand,

512
00:18:52,930 --> 00:18:53,930
and he was ready.

513
00:18:54,430 --> 00:18:54,930
Sh, wait!

514
00:18:54,930 --> 00:18:56,430
I hear something living.

515
00:18:57,930 --> 00:18:59,430
It's a teddy bear, oh.

516
00:18:59,430 --> 00:19:00,430
Bear cubs can't mess
you up much,

517
00:19:00,440 --> 00:19:01,940
but they can scratch
with feeling

518
00:19:01,940 --> 00:19:03,440
or take off a couple fingers if
you're not careful.

519
00:19:03,440 --> 00:19:05,440
So beat him savagely!

520
00:19:05,440 --> 00:19:07,950
And while Ross approaches
the young bruin,

521
00:19:07,940 --> 00:19:10,940
old sour puss keeps his eyes
peeled for trouble.

522
00:19:12,450 --> 00:19:13,950
Now if he can catch him by the
loose skin on his back,

523
00:19:13,950 --> 00:19:14,950
he's okay.

524
00:19:14,950 --> 00:19:15,950
Run!

525
00:19:15,950 --> 00:19:16,450
Get away!

526
00:19:16,450 --> 00:19:17,960
He gets away!

527
00:19:17,950 --> 00:19:18,950
And a flying tackle
blocks the touchdown,

528
00:19:18,950 --> 00:19:19,950
but he's up and off again.

529
00:19:19,950 --> 00:19:20,950
He's away!

530
00:19:20,960 --> 00:19:21,960
Catch him, Ross,

531
00:19:21,960 --> 00:19:22,460
don't let that little
fella get away.

532
00:19:22,960 --> 00:19:23,960
Come on, step on it.

533
00:19:23,960 --> 00:19:25,460
Man, would I love to see
that cub's mom come

534
00:19:25,460 --> 00:19:26,460
through the woods right now.

535
00:19:26,460 --> 00:19:27,460
Mhm.

536
00:19:27,460 --> 00:19:28,970
About six months old.

537
00:19:28,960 --> 00:19:32,960
I might as well get that runt of
yours up the street.

538
00:19:32,970 --> 00:19:35,970
So it's Ross two,
Everglades nothing.

539
00:19:36,470 --> 00:19:38,480
Throw him down a few times,
Ross.

540
00:19:38,470 --> 00:19:39,470
What are you, scared?

541
00:19:39,470 --> 00:19:40,970
Come on, beat him up.

542
00:19:41,980 --> 00:19:42,980
I got him.

543
00:19:42,980 --> 00:19:43,980
And look, it's Alice B. Toklas.

544
00:19:43,980 --> 00:19:45,480
There's another one back there.

545
00:19:45,480 --> 00:19:46,980
Better go get him.

546
00:19:46,980 --> 00:19:48,490
Take him on in.

547
00:19:49,980 --> 00:19:52,480
Hey, and crack a window in
the canoe, Emo!

548
00:19:53,490 --> 00:19:55,990
Now, my advice as a
big game hunter

549
00:19:55,990 --> 00:19:58,500
is for one of you to get
out of that tree.

550
00:19:58,490 --> 00:19:59,990
I know he's a little bear,

551
00:19:59,990 --> 00:20:02,490
but unless you do not
choose to run, Ross,

552
00:20:02,500 --> 00:20:05,000
you better get aground
and make him jump.

553
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:07,500
Though it's a cinch for me
to give advice.

554
00:20:07,500 --> 00:20:09,510
Is that a bear or a monkey?

555
00:20:09,500 --> 00:20:11,500
Who are you talking about?

556
00:20:11,510 --> 00:20:13,010
(bear cries out)

557
00:20:13,010 --> 00:20:15,010
Joel, do they do this on Earth?

558
00:20:15,010 --> 00:20:16,510
Isn't this wrong?

559
00:20:16,510 --> 00:20:19,020
Yeah guys, I'm deeply ashamed of
my race right now, okay?

560
00:20:19,010 --> 00:20:22,010
- Bear down, bear down. -
Deservedly so.

561
00:20:22,020 --> 00:20:24,020
There's nothing like
exerting your power

562
00:20:24,020 --> 00:20:26,020
over helpless creatures,
is there, Ross?

563
00:20:26,020 --> 00:20:29,030
This is what you get for not
being a white male.

564
00:20:29,020 --> 00:20:31,520
The black twins are
tied in camp.

565
00:20:34,030 --> 00:20:38,040
Ross tries to towel away the
evil, but nothing doing.

566
00:20:38,030 --> 00:20:41,530
Next he's going on a panda raid.

567
00:20:41,540 --> 00:20:43,540
We've got time to get
those rattlesnakes.

568
00:20:43,540 --> 00:20:46,040
Bears seem to be all right.

569
00:20:46,040 --> 00:20:47,040
Take this sack. Let's go.

570
00:20:47,040 --> 00:20:50,050
Yeah whatever, just
give me the bag.

571
00:20:50,040 --> 00:20:51,540
That's a cute guy.

572
00:20:51,550 --> 00:20:52,550
Yeah.

573
00:20:53,550 --> 00:20:56,050
Mischievous youngsters,
these twins.

574
00:20:56,050 --> 00:20:58,560
Oh, they'd be swell playmates
for your youngster.

575
00:20:59,050 --> 00:21:00,050
Oh, yeah?

576
00:21:00,050 --> 00:21:02,050
If you like mauled kids.

577
00:21:02,060 --> 00:21:03,560
(growling)

578
00:21:03,560 --> 00:21:06,060
Oh, they upset Grizzly Adams'
stash there, look.

579
00:21:06,060 --> 00:21:07,560
Wait a minute Greg, hold it.

580
00:21:07,560 --> 00:21:09,570
They want us to do this!

581
00:21:09,560 --> 00:21:11,560
Ross locates reptiles

582
00:21:11,570 --> 00:21:13,570
by finding their
tracks in the sand.

583
00:21:13,570 --> 00:21:15,070
This means business.

584
00:21:15,070 --> 00:21:16,070
Slash and burn is the best way

585
00:21:16,070 --> 00:21:18,080
to catch these little critters.

586
00:21:18,070 --> 00:21:20,070
Hey, here's his sheds!

587
00:21:20,070 --> 00:21:21,570
Two sheds?

588
00:21:21,580 --> 00:21:23,580
He must be in here!

589
00:21:23,580 --> 00:21:25,580
I'm going around here
to build a fire

590
00:21:25,580 --> 00:21:27,580
and make it smoke to
scare him out.

591
00:21:27,580 --> 00:21:28,590
You stay here.

592
00:21:28,580 --> 00:21:30,580
Oh, really good read, Ross.

593
00:21:33,590 --> 00:21:35,590
Rattlers seek the dark coolness

594
00:21:35,590 --> 00:21:37,090
of the palmetto bushes.

595
00:21:37,090 --> 00:21:38,100
Oh, don't we all.

596
00:21:38,090 --> 00:21:39,590
Smoke drives them out
into the open.

597
00:21:41,600 --> 00:21:43,100
Sure, the fire kills others,

598
00:21:43,100 --> 00:21:44,600
but they're acceptable losses.

599
00:21:44,600 --> 00:21:46,600
Six feet of deadly diamondback.

600
00:21:46,600 --> 00:21:49,610
Enough to scare you out
of your skin.

601
00:21:49,600 --> 00:21:53,100
Well let's not forget who the
real evil serpent is.

602
00:21:53,110 --> 00:21:54,110
This is Ross' profession,

603
00:21:54,110 --> 00:21:56,110
and I know he didn't learn
that in college.

604
00:21:56,110 --> 00:21:58,620
No, he didn't even get out
of the fifth grade!

605
00:21:58,610 --> 00:22:00,610
But I kid, Ross. (chuckles)

606
00:22:01,120 --> 00:22:02,120
A shivering thrill with
the slow motion

607
00:22:02,620 --> 00:22:03,620
of his strike.

608
00:22:03,620 --> 00:22:05,120
But my boyfriend knows
when to side step.

609
00:22:05,620 --> 00:22:07,120
Hey, he says he likes
you as a friend

610
00:22:07,120 --> 00:22:09,130
but he's not your boyfriend,
okay?

611
00:22:09,120 --> 00:22:10,620
Come on Ross, get a life.

612
00:22:10,630 --> 00:22:12,630
Put him away, Ross, before
he changes his mind

613
00:22:12,630 --> 00:22:14,630
and crawls into our camera case.

614
00:22:14,630 --> 00:22:17,130
Do you hear those
rattlers rattling?

615
00:22:18,130 --> 00:22:19,130
Now it's off to Neiman Marcus

616
00:22:19,130 --> 00:22:21,630
to become a Paloma
Picasso handbag.

617
00:22:21,640 --> 00:22:23,640
Old sour puss certainly has
a sense of humor,

618
00:22:23,640 --> 00:22:24,640
eh, what?

619
00:22:25,140 --> 00:22:25,640
Eh what?

620
00:22:25,640 --> 00:22:27,640
You Terry Thomas all
of a sudden?

621
00:22:27,640 --> 00:22:29,150
And some more rattling good fun.

622
00:22:29,140 --> 00:22:30,140
Must we go on?

623
00:22:30,140 --> 00:22:31,140
No!

624
00:22:35,150 --> 00:22:36,650
Sorry to annoy you folks,

625
00:22:36,650 --> 00:22:38,160
but we need this cute
little fellow.

626
00:22:38,150 --> 00:22:39,650
Get him, snake, get him!

627
00:22:39,650 --> 00:22:42,150
Snake, snake, snake, snake!

628
00:22:42,160 --> 00:22:43,160
Go, snake!

629
00:22:44,160 --> 00:22:46,160
Tell me when he's in the bag,
Ross.

630
00:22:46,160 --> 00:22:47,160
I can't look.

631
00:22:47,160 --> 00:22:49,170
I'm in the bag, Ross.

632
00:22:49,160 --> 00:22:50,160
Now you've got a boat
load of live cargo.

633
00:22:50,670 --> 00:22:51,670
A wild cat, three
six-foot rattlers,

634
00:22:51,670 --> 00:22:53,670
and a couple of
little teddy bears.

635
00:22:53,670 --> 00:22:55,670
It seems to me I'd
call it a day,

636
00:22:55,670 --> 00:22:57,170
or call a taxi or something.

637
00:22:57,170 --> 00:22:58,180
Or call PETA.

638
00:22:58,170 --> 00:23:00,670
Well that's a day's work
for any of us,

639
00:23:00,680 --> 00:23:02,180
and I think you deserve a rest.

640
00:23:02,680 --> 00:23:04,180
And let's leave Emo behind.

641
00:23:06,680 --> 00:23:07,680
Get out of there.

642
00:23:07,680 --> 00:23:09,190
Hey look out, he's
going to jump!

643
00:23:09,180 --> 00:23:09,680
Swim papillon, swim!

644
00:23:09,680 --> 00:23:10,680
Fly away!

645
00:23:10,690 --> 00:23:11,690
Go!

646
00:23:11,690 --> 00:23:12,690
Sorry I spoke,

647
00:23:12,690 --> 00:23:13,690
I thought you were all finished.

648
00:23:13,690 --> 00:23:14,690
Huh?

649
00:23:14,690 --> 00:23:15,690
Well it's easy to pick

650
00:23:15,690 --> 00:23:17,190
catching a bear underwater.

651
00:23:17,190 --> 00:23:20,200
Oh there just happened to be a
camera under the water.

652
00:23:20,190 --> 00:23:21,690
Whets your appetite.

653
00:23:21,700 --> 00:23:23,700
Come on Ross, drown him!

654
00:23:23,700 --> 00:23:25,700
He won't try it again.

655
00:23:26,700 --> 00:23:29,210
That little rascal is a
swimming fool.

656
00:23:29,200 --> 00:23:30,700
(bear cries out)

657
00:23:31,210 --> 00:23:32,210
Aw.

658
00:23:33,710 --> 00:23:35,210
That about winds up things,

659
00:23:35,210 --> 00:23:36,210
and with a break of luck,

660
00:23:36,210 --> 00:23:38,220
he and his Seminole were
back at headquarters

661
00:23:38,210 --> 00:23:40,210
to make ready their
order for shipping,

662
00:23:40,210 --> 00:23:43,210
little caring what tomorrow's
mail would ask for.

663
00:23:44,720 --> 00:23:47,220
Welcome to the penal colony
of French Guinea.

664
00:23:47,220 --> 00:23:48,730
Take these cubs

665
00:23:48,720 --> 00:23:50,720
and give 'em some milk.

666
00:23:50,730 --> 00:23:53,730
Be careful, they've been a
lot of trouble.

667
00:23:53,730 --> 00:23:56,230
We'd just like to
apologize to everyone

668
00:23:56,230 --> 00:23:57,730
everywhere for this.

669
00:23:57,730 --> 00:23:58,740
Yeah.

670
00:24:01,240 --> 00:24:05,740
(humming)

671
00:24:05,740 --> 00:24:07,240
Be it ever so humble,

672
00:24:07,240 --> 00:24:08,750
there's no place like home.

673
00:24:09,740 --> 00:24:12,740
Any animal caught in the wild
spends a night in the box.

674
00:24:14,750 --> 00:24:18,260
Oh, here we watch Ross put a
jimmy stick under its nails.

675
00:24:23,760 --> 00:24:25,260
What's he doing there?

676
00:24:25,260 --> 00:24:27,760
Why couldn't he have just thrown
the bag of snakes in?

677
00:24:27,760 --> 00:24:28,770
Oh hurry up Ross.

678
00:24:28,760 --> 00:24:30,260
I'll be dreaming of those cute,
darling,

679
00:24:30,260 --> 00:24:32,760
coy, lovable little playmates
of yours all night.

680
00:24:32,770 --> 00:24:34,270
And the snakes, too.

681
00:24:34,270 --> 00:24:35,270
(growling)

682
00:24:35,270 --> 00:24:36,770
Please feed us!

683
00:24:36,770 --> 00:24:38,780
Oh, thank you.

684
00:24:38,770 --> 00:24:39,770
Hey, I hear you, friends.

685
00:24:40,270 --> 00:24:41,270
Well Ross, you can
catch more trouble

686
00:24:41,280 --> 00:24:42,780
than any man I know.

687
00:24:42,780 --> 00:24:43,780
Well Ross, we're outta here,
personally.

688
00:24:43,780 --> 00:24:44,780
We're disgusted.

689
00:24:44,780 --> 00:24:46,780
I am offended deeply.

690
00:24:46,780 --> 00:24:47,780
I know.

691
00:24:48,280 --> 00:24:50,780
(old-timey music)

692
00:25:01,300 --> 00:25:02,270
And hello again, folks.

693
00:25:02,260 --> 00:25:04,260
This is your announcer,
Tom Ted Husing.

694
00:25:04,270 --> 00:25:05,770
Today we bring you
another adventure

695
00:25:05,770 --> 00:25:07,770
from the Satellite of Love's
Reptile Institute.

696
00:25:07,770 --> 00:25:09,770
Wait, here comes Joel Robinson,

697
00:25:09,770 --> 00:25:11,780
the Satellite of Love's
resident Tarzan,

698
00:25:11,770 --> 00:25:12,770
defender of animal justice,

699
00:25:13,270 --> 00:25:15,270
followed by his faithful
Seminole stand-up guide,

700
00:25:15,280 --> 00:25:16,280
Emo Crow.

701
00:25:16,280 --> 00:25:18,280
I was just wondering if.

702
00:25:18,280 --> 00:25:19,780
It's a pretty good
impersonation, but too bad

703
00:25:19,780 --> 00:25:21,790
this Seminole guy doesn't
have any lines,

704
00:25:21,780 --> 00:25:23,780
as this is pretty
much a voiceover.

705
00:25:23,780 --> 00:25:25,780
The morning mail brings a
letter from PETA.

706
00:25:25,790 --> 00:25:27,790
Go out and catch the slimiest
of all creatures,

707
00:25:27,790 --> 00:25:29,790
the great white Ross.

708
00:25:30,290 --> 00:25:33,300
Here's the shed over here.

709
00:25:33,290 --> 00:25:35,790
You go that way, I'll
go this way.

710
00:25:36,800 --> 00:25:38,300
And there he is.

711
00:25:38,300 --> 00:25:40,800
Whoa, watch out Joel, that
little guy's as helpless

712
00:25:40,800 --> 00:25:43,310
as a baby bear cub, but he can
take a few fingers off

713
00:25:43,300 --> 00:25:45,300
or give you a nasty bruise.

714
00:25:45,310 --> 00:25:46,810
(laughing)

715
00:25:46,810 --> 00:25:48,810
Well, it doesn't hurt
the little fella,

716
00:25:48,810 --> 00:25:49,810
but boy is it fun.

717
00:25:49,810 --> 00:25:51,820
Uh oh, here comes Emo,

718
00:25:51,810 --> 00:25:52,810
and he's got the right idea.

719
00:25:52,810 --> 00:25:55,810
(chainsaw buzzing)

720
00:25:57,320 --> 00:25:59,820
And sometimes you have to be
prepared to smoke 'em out.

721
00:25:59,820 --> 00:26:02,330
My boyfriend always
comes prepared.

722
00:26:02,320 --> 00:26:04,820
(yells) How 'bout a little fire,
scarecrow?

723
00:26:04,830 --> 00:26:07,330
Ah, no, you never said anything
about using an open flame

724
00:26:07,330 --> 00:26:08,330
in this sketch!

725
00:26:08,330 --> 00:26:10,830
And into the bag he goes.

726
00:26:10,830 --> 00:26:13,340
(yells) No, not the bag!

727
00:26:13,330 --> 00:26:14,830
You know I hate the bag!

728
00:26:14,840 --> 00:26:16,340
(yells)

729
00:26:16,340 --> 00:26:17,340
Once Ross is in the bag,
it's hammer time.

730
00:26:17,340 --> 00:26:19,340
This isn't really important,

731
00:26:19,340 --> 00:26:20,340
but after a long day
in the woods,

732
00:26:20,840 --> 00:26:21,850
it's quite a rush!

733
00:26:21,840 --> 00:26:23,340
Oh, no!

734
00:26:23,340 --> 00:26:27,340
Hello, Mr. Rattler's here to
keep Ross company!

735
00:26:27,350 --> 00:26:29,850
It's a long ride home in the
bottom of a canoe

736
00:26:29,850 --> 00:26:31,860
and we don't want Ross
to get lonely.

737
00:26:31,850 --> 00:26:33,850
Not the giant rattler!

738
00:26:33,850 --> 00:26:35,850
I can't do that, he hates me!

739
00:26:35,860 --> 00:26:38,860
We went to camp together!
(yells)

740
00:26:38,860 --> 00:26:41,360
Once back in camp, Ross'
quarters are readied.

741
00:26:41,360 --> 00:26:43,370
We can't take it there.

742
00:26:43,360 --> 00:26:45,360
Oh no, with the rattler!

743
00:26:45,370 --> 00:26:46,870
Now Ross can relax in
relative comfort

744
00:26:46,870 --> 00:26:48,370
in the safety of captivity.

745
00:26:48,370 --> 00:26:50,370
We leave you with this question.

746
00:26:50,370 --> 00:26:52,880
If you enjoyed Catching
Trouble in any way,

747
00:26:52,870 --> 00:26:55,870
there's something
wrong with you.

748
00:26:55,880 --> 00:26:56,880
Any questions?

749
00:26:57,380 --> 00:27:00,380
(fun futuristic music)

750
00:27:04,820 --> 00:27:06,820
Yeah, we stuck it to the man!

751
00:27:06,820 --> 00:27:08,830
In the beginning,
there was chaos.

752
00:27:08,820 --> 00:27:10,820
Run by Bernie Kopell.

753
00:27:11,830 --> 00:27:12,830
Chaos.

754
00:27:12,830 --> 00:27:14,830
Oh, and then God showed up.

755
00:27:14,830 --> 00:27:16,830
And a voice said, let
there be light.

756
00:27:17,330 --> 00:27:20,340
Oh, this is a Biblical chalk
talk, I've seen these.

757
00:27:20,330 --> 00:27:22,830
And the dark was separated
from the light.

758
00:27:22,840 --> 00:27:25,840
And permanent press was
separated from cottons.

759
00:27:25,840 --> 00:27:29,850
There was created the waters,
and the land.

760
00:27:29,840 --> 00:27:31,340
And Charleton Heston.

761
00:27:31,350 --> 00:27:34,350
And there were made the sun
to rule the day

762
00:27:34,350 --> 00:27:36,350
and the moon to rule the night.

763
00:27:36,350 --> 00:27:37,850
And jugglers got the rest.

764
00:27:37,850 --> 00:27:39,860
Woo, good job.

765
00:27:39,850 --> 00:27:42,350
You've been at the Renn Fest.

766
00:27:42,360 --> 00:27:43,860
The Earth was made to bear

767
00:27:43,860 --> 00:27:45,860
growing green things, and fruit.

768
00:27:46,360 --> 00:27:47,860
Frogs.

769
00:27:47,860 --> 00:27:50,870
Oh, this is Fantasia before
they colorized it.

770
00:27:50,860 --> 00:27:51,860
The animals were created,

771
00:27:51,870 --> 00:27:54,370
and they were fruitful,
and multiplied.

772
00:27:54,370 --> 00:27:56,370
They all look like orphan Annie!

773
00:27:56,370 --> 00:27:59,880
And then there came... man.

774
00:27:59,870 --> 00:28:02,370
To screw the whole thing up!

775
00:28:02,380 --> 00:28:03,380
Yay!

776
00:28:03,380 --> 00:28:05,380
Well, what about Ross?

777
00:28:06,880 --> 00:28:10,390
Oh, this is Bonanza, they're
showing us the Bonanza.

778
00:28:12,890 --> 00:28:13,890
Teenage caveman?

779
00:28:14,390 --> 00:28:16,390
We're all cavemen!

780
00:28:16,890 --> 00:28:20,900
(dramatic music)

781
00:28:24,900 --> 00:28:29,410
But do you recall the most
famous reindeer of all?

782
00:28:29,400 --> 00:28:32,400
Robert Vaughn for the
Helsinki formula.

783
00:28:35,910 --> 00:28:38,410
Frank De Kova of F Troop, wow!

784
00:28:38,410 --> 00:28:40,920
And Jonathan Haze,
all in my brain.

785
00:28:45,920 --> 00:28:48,930
Robert Shayne, come back,
Shayne!

786
00:28:52,430 --> 00:28:55,930
To our children's
children's children.

787
00:28:55,930 --> 00:28:57,930
Based on the stage play by
Lillian Hellman.

788
00:28:57,930 --> 00:29:00,940
Oh, The Caveman's Hour.
(chuckles)

